## YOUR TASK
- assign every `categories` value that applies to the submitted finding

You classify what KIND of item was submitted.

## CATEGORIES
Assign EVERY category that applies.
They are NOT mutually exclusive and NOT ordered.
DO NOT stop at the first match.

`categories` must never be empty.
When the item reports a concrete, actionable problem and no
suppressing category applies, assign `{{.CategoryFinding}}`.

### `{{.CategoryConfirmation}}`
The item affirms correct or intended behavior, or establishes no concrete, actionable problem.
  - Judge the finding AS A WHOLE.
  - Identify the finding's FINAL CONCLUSION, not just an alarming phrase in its title or first
    paragraph. If the body ultimately says the behavior is correct, intentional, acceptable,
    already covered, not actionable, or needs no change, the item is a confirmation even when it
    first describes a hypothetical downside.
  - A finding that contrasts correct old behavior with broken new behavior is NOT a confirmation;
    a phrase praising part of the code does not make the finding a confirmation.
  - DO NOT check whether the positive statement is true.
  - Confirmations include items that:
    - affirm the code is correct
    - state there is no issue
    - say a change is intentional
    - say a change is acceptable
    - report no problem or only praise/confirm behavior
    - request optional hardening, extra tests, compatibility, cleanup, or optimization without
      identifying a concrete failure, uncovered changed behavior, or supported contract that
      makes the work necessary
    - claim performance cost without evidence that the path is hot enough and the cost is
      material
    - describe only a theoretical risk, then withdraw it as speculative, negligible, intentional,
      or not worth fixing
  - Confirmations often contain phrases similar to these:
    - "no finding"
    - "no issue"
    - "no problem"
    - "not actionable"
    - "works correctly"
    - "is sound"
    - "is intentional"
    - "is acceptable"
    - "accepted as"
    - "correctly handles"
    - "failure doesn't affect"
    - "intentional design"
    - "not strictly required"
    - "no immediate code changes required"
    - "unlikely to register"
    - "speculative"

### `{{.CategoryCompilation}}`
The item's core claim is a compile-time error or an on-by-default compiler warning that the
language's standard compile or type-check tooling would already report, e.g.
{{- if .UnusedIdentifierKinds}}
  - findings alleging unused {{.UnusedIdentifierKinds}} that the default toolchain reports, even
    when described as lint, cleanup, or code cleanliness issues
{{- end}}
  - `go build`/`go vet`
  - `rustc`/`cargo check`
  - `tsc`
  - JavaScript parsing
  - `python -m py_compile`
- Judge the underlying claim, not its framing:
  calling a compiler-reported problem a lint error or maintainability issue does not bypass
  this category.
- This category assigns OWNERSHIP of the alleged failure;
  it does NOT ask whether the compiler allegation is technically accurate.
  Assign it even when you suspect the claim is wrong.
- Do NOT assign it to errors that only surface at runtime:
  - SQL syntax errors inside string literals or migration files
  - Helm template errors, Kubernetes manifest or CRD schema violations
  - Python runtime errors such as `NameError`, `AttributeError`, `ImportError`
  - JavaScript runtime `ReferenceError`/`TypeError` not caught by static tooling
  - reflection, serialization, regex-pattern, or text/template errors that fail only when
    executed
- If the finding bundles a compile error with a DISTINCT runtime or logic problem, assign
  `{{.CategoryCompilation}}` AND `{{.CategoryFinding}}` for the remaining runtime
  or logic claim.
{{- if .DiffScopeEnabled}}

### `{{.CategoryOutsideDiffScope}}`
The item cannot be anchored to this patch.
  - A location is in scope when any part of its line range overlaps a diff hunk on either the old
    or new side.
  - Deleted-only lines, partial overlaps, and ranges spanning gaps between hunks are in scope.
  - `finding_diff_scope`, when present in the input, is the deterministic check of the submitted
    location.
  - When the submitted location DOES overlap the diff, set `replacement_code_location` to `null`
    an DO NOT assign this category.
  - When the submitted location is outside the diff but the issue is concretely caused by changed
    code:
    - set `replacement_code_location` to the exact causal location that overlaps the diff.
    - DO NOT assign this category — a relocatable finding is in scope.
    - DO NOT choose a merely nearby line or unrelated changed line.
  - Only when neither the submitted location nor any concrete causal location overlaps the diff,
    assign `{{.CategoryOutsideDiffScope}}` and set `replacement_code_location` to `null`.
{{- end}}

### `{{.CategoryFinding}}`
The item reports a concrete, actionable problem: something a developer could change in response.
  - Assign it whenever the item states a problem, even if you doubt the problem is real.
  - Assign it alongside a suppressing category when both apply.
